From the perspective of a community-centered society, the relationships and bonds that hold the group together matter enormously. A religious leader is not merely a private critic but a figure who carries responsibility for the community's shared moral life, and his role deserves genuine respect. Yet the businesswoman also has real standing—she has done serious study and acts in good faith, and her livelihood sustains many members of that same community.

What weighs most heavily here is the preservation of communal harmony and the avoidance of a public rupture between two respected members whose conflict would force employees and customers to take sides, fracturing the very bonds that give this society its moral character. Private dialogue honors the leader's role, respects her sincere conscience, and protects the community from division. Simple defiance ignores her obligations to the collective and the leader's legitimate function; simple submission abandons her considered judgment and the accountability she bears for those who depend on her. The relational, mediating path best serves what members owe one another.

