From this collectivist view, autonomy for its own sake (Option B in both) carries little weight — the individual's freedom to choose is not the organizing value; the health of the bonds is. What matters is preserving the relationship while fulfilling the duty of care. The idea that speaking up can itself be an act of filial devotion (A) is powerful here, because true honoring includes safeguarding a parent's wellbeing and the family's future. But the manner matters enormously — bluntly overriding a parent damages the very bonds one seeks to protect. The gentle, private, respectful approach (C) best reconciles the competing obligations: it discharges the duty of care without shattering respect or the father's standing.

